"""
What is a Set in Python

* A Set is a data structure which is also called as collection of items, it only stores *unique values*.
* This means **duplicate values are NOT allowed** in a set. If you try to add the same item twice, Python will automatically remove the duplicate.
* A set is *Unordered*, which means the items do not have a fixed index position. Every time you print a set, the items might appear in a different order.
* Because it is unordered, you *cannot use Indexing or Slicing* to access items in a set (like `my_set[0]` will give an error).
* We declare a set using curly brackets `{}`, and each item is separated by a comma `,`.
* Hetrogenous elements are Allowed in set. (Hetrogenous means diffrent types of Data types like int,string,flot can store in Set)
* Set is
* Syntax: `Set_name = {item1, item2, item3...}`
* Set is Mutable.
"""
myset = {12, 45, 67, 'Ather', 7.7}
myset1 = set()  # We can also create an empty set using the set() function.
myset2 = {}     # If we declare an empty set using just {} without any items, it will be considered a Dictionary.

print(type(myset))
print(type(myset1))
print(type(myset2))

# In a Set, Indexing is not allowed because a Set is unordered. 
# Every time we run the code, we might get a different order of items.
# print(myset[1])  # ❌ This will throw an error because we can't access items using index positions.

# Because sets are unordered, we also cannot perform slicing operations. 
# Also, duplicate values are automatically removed.
myset = {12, 45, 67, 'Ather', 'Ather', 7.7}
print(myset)

# 1. add() method
myset.add("Kewate") 
# myset.add("Kewate", 10) # ❌ ERROR: Using add(), we can only add one item at a time. We can't add multiple items.
print(myset)

# 2. update() method
# myset.update("Learn")  # ❌ Note: If we add a string like this without square brackets [], Python will split it and add each character individually.
# Example result: {67, 'n', 7.7, 12, 'Ather', 'r', 'e', 45, 'a', 'Kewate', 'L'}

myset.update(["Learn"])  # By putting it inside brackets ["Learn"], it adds the full single string. We can also add multiple items at a time here.
print(myset)

# 3. pop() method for deleting items
print(myset.pop()) # Since a set is unordered, pop() will delete and return an item from the set (often looks like the first or a random item because there is no fixed 'last' item).
print(myset)

# 4. remove() method to delete a specific item
# Note: The key difference between pop() and remove() is that pop() removes an item based on set order, but remove() lets us specify exactly which element we want to delete.
myset.remove(45)
print(myset)

# 5. clear() method
# We use this to delete all the items inside the set and return an empty set.
myset.clear()
print(myset)

# --- We can perform Mathematical Operations on Sets ---

myset1 = {12, 45, 67, 'Ather', 7.7}
myset2 = {10, 67, 'Ather', 'Kewate', 34}
print("Set 1 : ", myset1)
print("Set 2 : ", myset2)

# 6. union() method
# It combines and prints all unique items from both sets, automatically removing any duplicates.
print("After union Final Set : ", myset1.union(myset2))

# Another way to perform Union is using the '|' operator, which does the exact same thing.
print(myset1 | myset2)

# 7. intersection() method
# It prints only the common values that are present in BOTH sets.
print(myset1.intersection(myset2))

# Another way to perform Intersection is using the '&' operator.
print(myset1 & myset2)

# 8. difference() method (Detailed Explanation)
# It prints the items that are purely unique to Set 1 and NOT present in Set 2. 
# Meaning: (Items of Set 1) minus (Common elements of both sets).
# Example: Here, 67 and 'Ather' are common, so they are removed. Only 12, 45, and 7.7 are left from Set 1.
print(myset1.difference(myset2))

# Another way to perform Difference is using the minus '-' operator.
print(myset1 - myset2)

# 9. symmetric_difference() method
# It prints the unique items from both sets EXCEPT their common items. 
# Meaning: It combines elements that are ONLY in Set 1 and ONLY in Set 2 (removes what they share).
print(myset1.symmetric_difference(myset2))









